===Replay: Italy vs Spain===
The replay was played the day after the first game. The exhaustion and the injuries resulting from the first tie forced Italy and Spain to make four and seven changes respectively. Most notably, the experienced Zamora had to give way to the uncapped goalkeeper [[Juan José Nogués]]. Once again, the game was marred by controversial refereeing decisions. In the first five minutes, [[Crisant Bosch]] was hacked down by [[Eraldo Monzeglio]] in the penalty box. The penalty was not given, but the tackle resulted in the injury of Bosch. With no substitutes available, Spain had to play the remainder of the game with 10 men. [[Giuseppe Meazza]] scored soon after from a corner kick situation. In the second half, two Spanish goals were disallowed: one for offside and the other for a foul on a Spanish player. The referee, [[René Mercet]], was suspended in the aftermath of the tournament.<ref name="fiedler" />

